so we've been covering the two recent  airplane crashes of the Boeing 737 eight  max there was one in October of 2018  Lyon air which is an Indonesian airline  and then there was a second crash more  recently an Ethiopian Airlines crash and  similar patterns in both sort of erratic  altitude changes shortly after takeoff  and the planes are now grounded  indefinitely worldwide over what is  believed to be a software issue rather  than I guess what you would call an air  worthiness issue and this is really big  I mean it's tragic in that hundreds of  people died and that in and of itself no  matter what else is going on is a major  thing it's big because airlines are  starting to transition over to the 737  max and its various iterations the 737  has been the sort of workhorse airplane  for the planet for decades and  eventually the idea is that old 737s the  737-800 and other versions of it will be  replaced with these different versions  of the 737 max the max eight nine and  ten I believe they are 5000 of these new  737 maxes have been ordered so far 371  have been delivered so far all 371 are  currently grounded so the disruptive  effect to airline operations and the  people's travel is huge but of course  necessary because you've got to keep  people safe when you know that there's a  problem the United States Justice  Department has now issued multiple  subpoenas into how the 737 max obtained  FAA certification to begin with and how  they have marketed the planes to  Airlines this is now a criminal  investigation a question that they're  asking or want to answer is exactly how  did Boeing present the plane as safe and  what data was used was the data accurate  or could there be criminal wrongdoing in  what was done there we don't actually  know right now what criminal laws could  be at issue it's unclear but we will  ultimately see and this has now crossed  over into what is being called a  potential criminal investigation and  that in and of itself is  huge we're also now learning and this is  just stunning that the day before the  lion air crash there was a pilot who was  flying on the lion air airplane that  crashed in the cockpit but in the jump  seat in other words this pilot was not  working as a pilot on that flight but  was flying in the jump seat maybe to get  back to where they next had to fly out  of who-knows-what in any case that pilot  was aware of this particular problem  and knew exactly which system had to be  disabled in order to prevent the plane  from starting these erratic altitude  changes and he was in the cockpit when  this problem started on that same lion  air aircraft that ultimately crashed the  day before thanks to the luck of him  being in the cockpit he immediately told  the pilots that were in command which  system to disable and what to do and it  arguably we believe prevented the lion  air air plane from crashing the day  before it actually did next day the  different crew flies that same aircraft  this pilot with knowledge of exactly  which system needs to be disabled is not  flying is not in the cockpit and we know  that the plane crashed and killed  everybody that was on board we didn't  know this until recently and this  suggests and I don't know if this is  tragically or not that pilots may have  been more aware of exactly what this  risk was than has been initially  reported and that the crew that was  flying on the day of the lion air crash  simply didn't know which system it was  that needed to be disabled in order to  respond to this malfunction so listen I  mean I I try to be very careful not to  react more significantly than is  warranted by the facts commercial  aviation in general is extraordinarily  safe but if these planes were not  currently grounded I wouldn't be flying  on them right  now and I understand that statistically  up until their grounding most Boeing 737  eight max --is took off and landed  without incident completely safe safely  and more than likely if I were to fly on  one even without this issue being fixed  I would probably be fine but there's a  very specific problem that's been  identified there have been two crashes  and I wouldn't get on one of these  planes and I know last time I said this  people emailed in and said David you're  probably still fine and the truth is  that even if you were flying on an  Airbus 320 you'd still have a greater  chance of being killed while driving to  the airport that's fine but it's sort of  a non sequitur the risk of driving to  the airport is not affected by the risk  of what airline airliner you were going  to be flying on and if it weren't  grounded I wouldn't be flying right now  on the Boeing 737 max I am flying on one  of the older 737s in a couple of weeks  but not the max I'm flying on Delta to  Las Vegas I don't even think that they  are using the max but I think that my  conclusion is similar to most people in  the audience which is that they would  not fly on the max at this point in time  and I'm confident that this is going to  be figured out it's not going to make  the two accidents any less tragic  there's no way of undoing that and now  that this criminal component has been  introduced  who knows what direction the  investigation would take and what it  means for boeing and in the back of this  there's the political stuff that we know  Trump spoke to the Boeing CEO around the  time that the grounding decision was  made in the United States and there's  potentially all sorts of political stuff  at play if you opened the door to  suspicious FAA approvals although at  this point in time we have an inquiry  but we don't have evidence that anything  unusual happened in terms of the  approval and certification of this  particular aircraft  it'll be figured out when I don't know  send me your thoughts would you fly on a  Boeing 737 a max eight at this point in  time if it were not grounded I'm on  Twitter at D Pakman the show is on  Twitter at David Pakman show  one of today's david pakman show  sponsors is simple habit an app that  provides a massive library of really  excellent five minute guided meditations  they're letting everybody in our  audience use it completely free for one  week when you go to simple habit calm  slash Pakman we've talked a lot on the  program about the growing scientific  evidence pointing to the benefits of  meditation and what really sets simple  habit apart is the variety of guided  meditations that they offer more than  2,000 made for many different issues  that you may be dealing with if you need  help falling asleep if you have anxiety  that you're dealing with if you want to  be more productive during the day they  have meditations specifically tailored  for certain times of day like when  you're waking up or your commute to work  or when you've just gotten home from  work I'm a huge advocate of meditation  myself I've really enjoyed integrating  simple habit into my day and each  meditation takes just five minutes and  they're very easy to follow maybe you  want to feel more grounded before you  start your day at work or at school  maybe you want to keep your mind a  little more clear so that it's easier to  accomplish your goals or you just want a  calmer more peaceful experience  throughout your day simple habit can  help you get there it just won the 2018  Google Play award for best well-being  app it's available on Android iOS and  web browser and you can try it  completely free for a week check it out  go to simple habit calm slash Pakman  I've put a link down below underneath  the video

